---
import DefaultLayout from "@/layouts/default-layout.astro";

export const title = "Caesar";
export const summary =
    "A Caesar cipher lets you add an arbitrary value, shifting each letter forwards or backwards. Traditionally, the offset is 3, making A into D, B into E, etc.";
export const cipher = true;

const breadcrumbs = [
    { name: "Rumkin.com", href: "/" },
    { name: "Web-Based Tools", href: "/tools/" },
    { name: "Ciphers and Codes", href: "/tools/cipher/" },
];
---

<script>
    import "@/assets/cipher/cipher-example.ts";
    import "@/assets/cipher/caesar-cipher.ts";
</script>

<DefaultLayout title={title} summary={summary} breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}>
    <p>
        This is a standard Caesarian Shift cipher encoder, also known as a
        rot-N encoder. It's a style of substitution cipher where you can add
        one, two, or any number up to 25 to each letter and see how the string changes.
        This is an offshoot of the <a href="../rot13/">rot13</a>{" "}encoder. To
        perform this shift by hand, you could just write the alphabet on two
        strips of paper. Line them up so the top strip's A matches the bottom
        strip's D (for example) and then you can encode. A simple test to see
        how this works would be to use the alphabet as the input and see the
        output while changing the value of <code>N</code>.
    </p>

    <p>
        This sort of cipher is also known as a wheel cipher. An inner wheel
        has the alphabet around its edge, and that is placed
        upon an outer wheel, also with the alphabet going around it. You can
        rotate the wheels so that ABC lines up with any series of letters, such
        as BCD or even one much further along, like QRS. Caesar originally shifted
        by 3 letters, so A -&gt; D, B -&gt; E, and so on, which was good enough
        for that time.
    </p>

    <p>
        To encode something, just pick an <code>N</code> and type in your
        message. To decode something, set <code>N</code> to 26 minus the
        encryption <code>N</code> and it should be decoded for you. Alternatively, the <a
            href="../cryptogram-solver/">cryptogram solver</a
        >{" "}can help you manually solve ciphers that use this method.
    </p>

    <p>
        You can make the cipher more complicated by shuffling the alphabet
        with a key. Performing this by hand would be nearly the same as with a
        plain Caesar alphabet, but you would write the scrambled alphabet instead of
        the sorted alphabet on both the outer and inner wheel rings (or top and
        bottom pieces of paper).
    </p>

    <p>Examples:</p>

    <ul>
        <li>
            <cipher-example
                topic="caesar"
                label="Wikipedia"
                payload-alphabet="English alphabetKey: useLastInstance:false reverseKey:false reverseAlphabet:false keyAtEnd:false"
                payload-direction="ENCRYPT"
                payload-input="THE QUICK BROWN FOX JUMPS OVER THE LAZY DOG"
                payload-n="23"></cipher-example>
        </li>
        <li>
            <cipher-example
                topic="caesar"
                label="Cicada 3301"
                payload-alphabet="English alphabetKey: useLastInstance:false reverseKey:false reverseAlphabet:false keyAtEnd:false"
                payload-direction="DECRYPT"
                payload-input="lxxt>33m2mqkyv2gsq3q=w]O2ntk"
                payload-n="4"></cipher-example>
        </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        That last one is the first step of the Cicada 3301 puzzle of January
        2012. The image posted to 4chan had this text stuck on the end of the
        file, under the heading <q>TIBERIVS CLAVDIVS CAESAR says</q>, which is
        about as broad a hint as a puzzle ever gives.
    </p>

    <p>
        The letters come out as a URL and the punctuation does not, because
        3301 rotated an alphabet that included the digits and the symbols. A
        Caesar shift works on whatever alphabet you agree on beforehand; this
        page rotates the letters and leaves everything else alone, which is
        the usual convention and is why <code>://</code> is still{" "}
        <code>&gt;33</code> in the output.
    </p>

    <caesar-cipher></caesar-cipher>
</DefaultLayout>
